<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olaf II of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 995–1030</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Saint Olaf, Olaf Haraldsson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olaf Haraldsson, later Saint Olaf, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1015 to 1028. A former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raider, he drove out the earls of Lade, subdued the petty kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppland">Upplands</a> around 1018 and pressed the conversion of Norway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> drove him out in 1028, and he was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stiklestad">Battle of Stiklestad</a> in 1030 trying to regain the throne. Venerated as a saint, he became Norway's patron.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway.jpg" alt="Olaf II of Norway" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olav_der_Heilige07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
